{"skill": "Main Idea", "difficulty": "Hard", "question": "The following excerpt is from Charles Darwin's *On the Origin of Species* (1859):

"It may metaphorically be said that natural selection is daily and hourly scrutinizing, throughout the world, every variation, even the slightest; rejecting that which is bad, preserving and adding up all that is good; silently and insensibly working, whenever and wherever opportunity offers, at the improvement of each organic being in relation to its organic and inorganic conditions of life."

What does the passage mainly suggest about the process of natural selection?", "choices": ["A. Natural selection examines and eliminates only significant traits.", "B. Natural selection tirelessly sifts through all variations, favoring beneficial ones.", "C. The mechanism of natural selection is sporadic and location-specific.", "D. The process leads to uniform changes across all species."], "answer": "B", "explanation": "The passage illustrates the continuous and thorough nature of natural selection. It highlights how even slight variations are scrutinized, with negative traits discarded and positive traits accumulated over time, emphasizing its role in adaptation and improvement."}

