{"skill": "Inferences", "difficulty": "Medium", "question": "In *The Federalist No. 51*, James Madison writes: 

"If men were angels, no government would be necessary. If angels were to govern men, neither external nor internal controls on government would be necessary." 

Which inference is best supported by this excerpt?", "choices": ["A) Governments should be designed to encourage citizens to act as angels.", "B) Governments must have checks and balances because human nature is imperfect.", "C) Governments serve no significant purpose when citizens remain virtuous.", "D) Governments can be led only by citizens with perfect moral character."], "answer": "B", "explanation": "Madison highlights that people are not perfect and thus systems of governance must include safeguards against misuse of power. Since humans are not "angels," checks and balances are needed to regulate authority. The correct option carefully notes that the author refers to human imperfection and calls for structural protections, matching the main inference of the passage."}

